<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Seru Epenisa Cakobau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1815–<a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ratu Seru Cakobau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Seru Epenisa Cakobau was the paramount chief of Bau, a small island state off <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viti-levu">Viti Levu</a> that dominated much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a> in the mid-19th century. He converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in 1854, and in 1871 he became king of a united Kingdom of Fiji. Burdened by debts and unrest, he ceded Fiji to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1874.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/seru-epenisa-cakobau.jpg" alt="Seru Epenisa Cakobau" width="320" height="495" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francis Herbert Dufty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cakobau,_died_February_1883,_photograph_by_Francis_H._Dufty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Tanoa Visawaqa and his son <b>Seru Epenisa Cakobau</b>, Bau dominated much of eastern Fiji through tribute and war.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bau">Kingdom of Bau</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Seru Epenisa Cakobau</b> of the Kingdom of Bau claimed the title Tui Viti, king of Fiji, but faced rivals such as Enele Maʻafu of the Tovata Confederacy and American debt claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-fiji">Kingdom of Fiji</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rivalry with the Kingdom of Bau under <b>Cakobau</b> shaped Fijian politics.</blockquote><a href="/state/tovata-confederacy">Tovata Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nairai. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiji">Fiji</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-17.8,179.42&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Nairai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="9100 2824 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Seru Epenisa Cakobau"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M9869 3094h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M9869 3094h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-17.8,179.42&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
